# Vite fingerprints everything under /assets, so these URLs never change and can
# be cached for a year.
#
# They were capped at an hour for a reason worth keeping written down. If a
# request landed in the moment between a new index.html going live and its
# assets propagating, the SPA fallback answered with HTML at 200, and a long
# rule pinned that broken answer into the edge and browser caches. That took the
# site down on 2026-08-04, and an hour capped the damage.
#
# The fallback no longer does that: a missing file under /assets now returns 404
# rather than 200, so there is no wrong answer left to pin. Checked before
# changing this, against the live site:
#
#   curl -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' https://volobg3.com/assets/does-not-exist.js
#   404
#
# If that ever answers 200 again, put the hour back before anything else.
#
# The cost of the cap was real: Cloudflare reported 28.6 percent of requests
# served from cache, on a site that is entirely static.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The masterlist keeps a stable URL and changes when submissions merge. An hour
# of caching keeps it fast without holding users on stale data for long.
/bg3-masterlist.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/index.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
